1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,387
Cache-Busting Isn’t Proof: Read the Headers.

2
00:00:03,387 --> 00:00:11,000
Quick explainer on why ?cb= is just a hint, and how to confirm with HTTP headers instead of vibes.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:24,562
Hook: one tab loads https://example.com/page.html and looks old, another tab with ?cb=123 looks new.

4
00:00:24,562 --> 00:00:31,512
It feels like the query fixed it, but it might have just routed you to a different cache while stale content still exists elsewhere.

5
00:00:31,512 --> 00:00:44,200
Why cache-busting can mislead: adding a query can shift you to a different cache key, can still serve stale if that edge hasn’t revalidated, and it can hide split deployments because you only verified one variant.

6
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:49,887
Where caches sit: your browser cache, a CDN/edge cache, maybe an origin cache.

7
00:00:49,887 --> 00:00:53,062
Different edges can disagree for a few minutes.

8
00:00:53,062 --> 00:00:58,387
We won’t overclaim about any provider—just read what the response tells us.

9
00:00:58,387 --> 00:01:09,550
Minimal checklist: look at Status, Age, Cache-Control, ETag, Last-Modified, Via, and if present CF-Cache-Status or similar.

10
00:01:09,550 --> 00:01:16,237
Remember gzip/range pitfalls were covered in Video 2; today is about freshness signals.

11
00:01:16,237 --> 00:01:19,012
HEAD example to see metadata fast.

12
00:01:19,012 --> 00:01:19,675
Run:

13
00:01:19,675 --> 00:01:27,625
curl -I https://example.com/page.html

14
00:01:27,625 --> 00:01:36,212
Scan Status, Age, Cache-Control, ETag/Last-Modified, and any Via/CF-Cache-Status.

15
00:01:36,212 --> 00:01:44,700
A high Age suggests cached; Cache-Control: no-cache means it revalidates; weak ETags start with W/.

16
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:49,512
GET while dumping headers to stdout so you can see both headers and a bit of body:

17
00:01:49,512 --> 00:01:58,025
curl -D - https://example.com/page.html

18
00:01:58,025 --> 00:02:05,287
This shows any Age and ETag alongside the actual content, confirming whether the body matches the supposed version.

19
00:02:05,287 --> 00:02:09,950
Compare with and without a cache-bust to see if you are hitting different caches:

20
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:17,900
curl -I https://example.com/page.html

21
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:28,425
curl -I https://example.com/page.html?cb=now

22
00:02:28,425 --> 00:02:35,462
If one has Age: 0 and the other Age: 900, you’re seeing different cache states.

23
00:02:35,462 --> 00:02:39,762
That’s evidence of mixed state, not proof everything is fixed.

24
00:02:39,762 --> 00:02:59,137
How to read the headers: Status 200 vs 304 tells you if it was revalidated; Age shows cache time; Cache-Control hints on TTL and revalidation; ETag/Last-Modified identify versions; Via/CF-Cache-Status expose which layer served it.

25
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:02,162
Absence of a header is not proof of anything.

26
00:03:02,162 --> 00:03:09,550
Recap — four steps: 1) Run curl -I to see Status/Age/Cache-Control.

27
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:14,237
2) Run curl -D - for headers + body sanity check.

28
00:03:14,237 --> 00:03:19,987
3) Compare with and without ?cb= to detect split caches.

29
00:03:19,987 --> 00:03:29,650
4) If it differs, note URL, time, headers, and retest later; gzip/range caveats live in Video 2.

30
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:34,000
Closing: cache-busting is a probe, not a verdict.

31
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:41,937
Read headers, keep claims modest about CDNs, and document what you observe before declaring a deployment broken or fixed.

